module

public import FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«1066»

/-!
# Erdős Problem 1066, higher dimensions: there is no uniform lower bound `g_d(n) ≥ c n / d`

Generated by an AI agent and machine-checked; not reviewed by a human expert.

## Statement proved

`main` is the negation of the upstream statement `Erdos1066.erdos_1066.variants.higher_dim`
(Formal Conjectures, `FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1066.lean`, commit 9097e2f9, marked
`category research open`):

    ¬ ∃ c > (0 : ℝ), ∀ d n : ℕ, 1 ≤ d → c * n / d ≤ (Erdos1066.gDim d n : ℝ)

Here `gDim d n`, written `g_d(n)`, is the largest `m` such that every set of `n` points of `ℝ^d`
with pairwise distances at least `1` contains `m` points with pairwise distances greater than `1`.
The explicit form is `gDim_upper`: `g_d(n) ≤ 2A + 2` for `A = 2 ^ m`, `d = 6A²`, `n = 4A⁴`.
The axioms used are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.

## Proof outline

If `n` vectors of squared length `2A² + A` have pairwise inner products `±A`, there are at most two
distances, and inner product `+A` belongs to the smaller one. A subset in which every distance is
the larger one has all inner products `-A`, and `0 ≤ ‖∑ w‖²` shows that it has at most `2A + 2`
elements (Lemma 1). It remains to write down `4A⁴` such vectors in dimension `6A²`. Let `F` be the
field with `q = 2A²` elements, and let `W` be `F × F` with the addition
`(a, b) + (a', b') = (a + a', b + b' + a a')`. A character `χ` of `W` with `χ (0, 1) ≠ 1` has values
`±1, ±i` (Lemma 3). The functions `x ↦ χ (c x, b x)`, for `c, b ∈ F`, have inner products of
absolute value `√q` when the `c`'s differ, so their real parts are `±A` (Lemmas 2 and 4). They are
orthogonal when the `c`'s agree and the `b`'s differ; an additional entry `√A`, in a place that
depends on `c` only, turns this `0` into `A` (Lemma 5).

This file defines `W` over every field and keeps the signs in `c - c'`, `b - b'` and
`-(a, b) = (-a, -b + a²)`.

## Scope

* A refutation of a variant of Problem 1066, read as upstream reads it, with `c` independent of `d`
  and `n`. With a constant that may depend on `d` the bound is trivially true (bounded kissing
  number). The planar problem (the limit of `g(n)/n` in `ℝ^2`) is untouched.
* No defect is known in the upstream statement, and no degenerate case is used: the point sets are
  genuine 1-separated sets. They are explicit up to the choice of the field and of the character.
* The result is asymptotic in `d`. `gDim_upper` is better than the simplex bound `⌈n/(d+1)⌉` from
  `A = 4` (`n = 1024`, `d = 96`) on, and from `A = 8` on it is better than the ratio `n/(2d)` that
  Ismailescu, Lee and Lee reach for other `n` and `d`. At `A = 4` their configuration and de Caen's,
  both cited below, have more points in a smaller dimension with the same bound `10`.
* Not stated in Lean: `g_d(n) ≤ C n / d^(3/2)` for every `d ≥ 1` and some `n ≥ 1` (the text has an
  informal proof with `C = 118`; the earlier proof mentioned below was machine-checked with
  `C = 2^21`), failure for arbitrarily large `n` (far-apart copies), the consequence of de Caen's
  theorem, and the lower bound of order `d^(3/2)` for the chromatic number of
  minimum-distance graphs. The exponent `3/2` is not claimed to be optimal.

## Credit: what is borrowed

Such vectors are not new. D. de Caen, "Large equiangular sets of lines in Euclidean space",
Electron. J. Combin. 7 (2000) R55 (read), has `2(d+1)²/9` unit vectors in `ℝ^d`,
`d = 3 * 2^(2t-1) - 1`, with all inner products of distinct vectors `±1/(2^t + 1)`. Put
`A = 2^(t-1)` and multiply them by `√(A(2A+1))`: this gives `8A⁴` vectors in dimension `6A² - 1`
(twice as many as here, in one dimension fewer) with `‖w‖² = 2A² + A` and pairwise inner products
`±A`. Lemma 1 applies to them, so the result is an immediate corollary of a published theorem. The
proof below is self-contained.
Related:
* G. Greaves, J. H. Koolen, A. Munemasa and F. Szöllősi, "Equiangular lines in Euclidean spaces",
  https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.2155, Theorem 2.5: de Caen's parameters from mutually unbiased bases of
  `ℝ^(4A²)`, with extra coordinates of the kind used in Lemma 5.
* Such bases come from Kerdock sets (A. M. Kerdock 1972; P. J. Cameron and J. J. Seidel 1973; A. R.
  Calderbank, P. J. Cameron, W. M. Kantor and J. J. Seidel 1997). The form used here, characters of
  length-two Witt vectors (the group `W`), is the `ℤ/4` description of Kerdock codes of A. R.
  Hammons, P. V. Kumar, A. R. Calderbank, N. J. A. Sloane and P. Solé (1994). These four were not
  read for this work.
* An earlier AI-written proof (a random subset of a half-cube and a Kleitman-Winston count) came
  with the remark that "no published theorem was found that has the statement as a corollary"; de
  Caen's theorem had been missed. It also said that the explicit families checked, Kerdock's among
  them, are at most linear, which overlooks the extra coordinates of Lemma 5.

## Priority

New as far as known: no public proof or disproof of the higher-dimensional question, formal or
informal, was found in three searches on 24-25 Nov 2026, the last one with the vocabulary of
equiangular lines, two-graphs, Kerdock sets and mutually unbiased bases. The searches were limited,
so this means likely new, not certainly new. The result is so short a corollary of a theorem of 2000
that it may well be known to experts.
* Lemma 1 itself is in effect already used for one special system: in D. Ismailescu, S. Lee and
  T. Lee, https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23767 (18 Nov 2026), the points are `±v` for `r³ + 1` unit
  vectors in dimension `r² - r + 1` with inner products `±1/r` (Taylor's regular two-graphs), and
  the independence number is bounded by the same norm-of-the-sum argument. Equiangular lines and
  de Caen are not mentioned there, and the system gives only `n/α = 2d`. What was not found is the
  use of arbitrary large equiangular systems, which gives `n/α` of order `d^(3/2)`.
* G. Kalai, "Some old and new problems in combinatorial geometry I: Around Borsuk's problem", 2015,
  https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.04952, quotes de Caen's theorem (Theorem 2.6) and asks for the maximum
  chromatic number of minimum-distance ("kissing") graphs (Problem 5.1), but does not link the two.
* https://github.com/Dr-How/chromatic-sphere-packing (May 2026, a generated draft) conjectures the
  opposite: chromatic number `O(d)` for contact graphs of unit balls, which it calls equivalent to
  `α ≥ n/(Cd)`. The theorem refutes the latter, hence also the former.
* Not read: the problem page and forum thread on the Erdős problems site (not requested). Per
  https://github.com/teorth/erdosproblems (data/problems.yaml) the problem is open with four forum
  posts and no recorded proof claim, so a comment on this variant is not excluded.
  https://www.erdosproblemaday.com/problem/1066 (29 Jul 2026) lists it as open and says the official
  page then showed "a claimed proof or a listed worker", without details.
  https://github.com/conjectures-io/conjectures-validator lists this variant as an open target
  (28 Oct 2026). Some GitHub queries failed or were rate-limited. Google Scholar, the Isabelle AFP
  and the Coq archives were not checked.
* Not accessible in full: K. Bezdek, M. Naszódi and B. Visy, "On the mth Petty numbers of normed
  spaces", Discrete Geometry (2003) 291-304, the main unread source (it studies `g_d(n)` as
  `I_pack`); Erdős's letter; the books of Brass-Moser-Pach, Pach-Agarwal and Croft-Falconer-Guy; the
  Moser-Pach problem list; Raigorodskii's chapter in "Thirty Essays on Geometric Graph Theory".
  An expert should check these, and the four forum posts.

## Source of the problem and related work

* R. Pollack, "Increasing the minimum distance of a set of points", J. Combin. Theory Ser. A 40
  (1985) 450, reports a letter from Erdős: "Erdős asks for a proof or disproof of f(n,k) > cn/k"
  (`f(n,k)` is `g_k(n)`: `k` is the dimension and `n` the number of points). `main` refutes even the
  non-strict form of this inequality. He also gives
  `f(n,k) ≤ 6n/(6k+7)`, credited to Pach and independently to Graham and Chung.
* Formal statement: https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/pull/7482 (the reviewer
  noted that the constant is uniform in `d` and `n`). The earlier pull request 5876 was
  statement-only and was closed.
* A. Dumitrescu and Z. Lángi, "Avoiding Distances", https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24803 (Oct 2026):
  restates the question ("Erdős asked for a proof of f(n,d) ≥ cn/d"; Pollack has "proof or
  disproof"), with the simplex bound `⌈n/(d+1)⌉`.
* K. J. Swanepoel, "Combinatorial distance geometry in normed spaces",
  https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.00066, sec. 4.3: survey; the Euclidean bounds there are linear in `d`.
* Linear lower bounds for the chromatic number of ball packings, all of them also of the form
  `n/α` (number of points over independence number): H. Chen, https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.02070
  (2015), with `n/α = r³ + 1` for `d = r³ - r² + r` and `n = (r + 1)(r³ + 1)`, `r` a prime power, a
  table of single examples such as `n/α = 55` in dimension 22 (from the McLaughlin graph) and the
  conjecture, not reached here, that the chromatic number can be at least `c^√d`; D. Ismailescu,
  S. Lee and T. Lee, https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23767 (18 Nov 2026), with `n/α = 2d` for
  `d = r² - r + 1` and `n = 2(r³ + 1)`, `r` a power of an odd prime (`n = 1460`, `d = 73`, `α = 10`
  for `r = 9`). See also H. Chen, https://mathoverflow.net/questions/184908, with an answer of
  Kalai; Chen attributes the question for ball packings to Bagchi and Datta (2013). A question of
  K. Cantwell, https://mathoverflow.net/questions/8232, asserts a quadratic lower bound without
  proof; no support for it was found.
* Other Lean work on `g_d`: https://github.com/rjwalters/lean-genius has lower bounds `n/(τ(d)+1)`
  only. Other repositories treat the planar problem. None of it is used here.

No printed statement that contradicts the theorem was found.

